## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical ability to extend a docker-dca setup with a compliant, low-latency data-redaction path.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config-driven per-tenant policies
- Sidecar pattern and in-memory caching
- Streaming JSON redaction without full buffering
- Nginx-edge integration and TLS context

## Code Example
```go
package main
import (
  "encoding/json"
  "fmt"
)
func redact(m map[string]interface{}, fields map[string]bool){
  for k,v := range m {
    if fields[k] { m[k] = "***REDACTED***"; continue }
    if mm, ok := v.(map[string]interface{}); ok { redact(mm, fields) }
    if arr, ok := v.([]interface{}); ok {
      for _, it := range arr { if sub, ok := it.(map[string]interface{}); ok { redact(sub, fields) } }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test nested JSON redaction with large bodies?
- How would you monitor redaction performance without affecting latency?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to wire config-driven per-tenant controls in a multi-DC docker-dca setup, with TLS edge and simple runtime enforcement.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config for runtime limits
- Per-tenant rate limiting (token bucket)
- TLS via Traefik edge proxy
- Basic cross-DC deployment considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: token bucket per tenant with 60 rpm
const limits = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('/etc/config/tenant-rate-limits.json','utf8'));
const buckets = new Map();
function allow(tenant){
  const now = Date.now();
  let b = buckets.get(tenant);
  if(!b){ b = {tokens: limits[tenant]||0, last: now}; buckets.set(tenant,b); }
  // simplistic: refill 1 token per second for 60s window
  const elapsed = (now - b.last)/1000; b.last = now; b.tokens = Math.min((limits[tenant]||0), b.tokens + elapsed);
  if(b.tokens >= 1){ b.tokens--; return true; }
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you safely refresh Docker Config without restarting all containers?
- What edge cases arise with clock skew or config changes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests secure, rotating certs in Swarm with Vault, handling cert storage (secret vs volume), and canary semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Swarm multi-node with TLS and mTLS
- Vault PKI for dynamic certs
- Certs via writable volume + sidecar refresh
- Canary rollout with health checks and rollback
- Nginx TLS termination and service-to-service mTLS

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple cert refresh watcher from Vault to a mounted volume
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const fs = require('fs');
async function refresh() {
  const res = await fetch('https://vault.example/v1/pki/issue/api', { headers: { 'X-Vault-Token': process.env.VAULT_TOKEN } });
  const cert = await res.text();
  fs.writeFileSync('/certs/api.crt', cert);
}
setInterval(refresh, 5 * 60 * 1000);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate cert rotation does not break existing connections?
- How would you monitor Vault token renewal and automatic revocation?
